There's always the (remote) possibility that this `read()` will return an empty `shared_ptr`.  So my personal preference is to check for `nullptr` before proceeding.  Maybe something like this:
```
    auto const sleBuyer = view().read(keylet::account(buyer));
    if (!sleBuyer)
        return tecINTERNAL;
```

I was a bit bothered that the code inserted into `acceptOffer` and `doApply` was very close to identical.  So I did a bit of nosing about.  It looks to me that the code that actually transfers the token in `acceptOffer` and `doApply` could be a common helper function.  It might look like this:
```
TER
NFTokenAcceptOffer::transferNFToken(
    AccountID const& buyer, AccountID const& seller, uint256 const& nftokenID)
{
    auto tokenAndPage = nft::findTokenAndPage(view(), seller, nftokenID);

    if (!tokenAndPage)
        return tecINTERNAL;

    if (auto const ret = nft::removeToken(
            view(), seller, nftokenID, std::move(tokenAndPage->page));
        !isTesSuccess(ret))
        return ret;

    auto const sleBuyer = view().read(keylet::account(buyer));
    if (!sleBuyer)
        return tecINTERNAL;

    std::uint32_t const buyerOwnerCountBefore =
        sleBuyer->getFieldU32(sfOwnerCount);

    auto const insertRet =
        nft::insertToken(view(), buyer, std::move(tokenAndPage->token));

    // if fixNFTokenReserve is enabled, check if the buyer has sufficient
    // reserve to own a new object, if their OwnerCount changed.
    //
    // There was an issue where the buyer accepts a sell offer, the ledger
    // didn't check if the buyer has enough reserve, meaning that buyer can get
    // NFTs free of reserve.
    if (view().rules().enabled(fixNFTokenReserve))
    {
        // To check if there is sufficient reserve, we cannot use mPriorBalance
        // because NFT is sold for a price. So we must use the balance after
        // the deduction of the potential offer price. A small caveat here is
        // that the balance has already deducted the transaction fee, meaning
        // that the reserve requirement is a few drops higher.
        auto const buyerBalance = sleBuyer->getFieldAmount(sfBalance);

        auto const buyerOwnerCountAfter = sleBuyer->getFieldU32(sfOwnerCount);
        if (buyerOwnerCountAfter > buyerOwnerCountBefore)
        {
            if (auto const reserve =
                    view().fees().accountReserve(buyerOwnerCountAfter);
                buyerBalance < reserve)
                return tecINSUFFICIENT_RESERVE;
        }
    }

    return insertRet;
}
```
Consider replacing `checkBuyerReserve()` with a function like this.  Then you'll be able to reduce the size of both `acceptOffer` and `doApply` and increase confidence that they are both doing the same thing more often.

If you are curious, you can see an example of what I'm describing as the top-most commit here: https://github.com/scottschurr/rippled/commits/shawnx-nft-reserve

The lambda / `tuple` / structured binding approach is very much how the cool kids do it these days.  And it works.

I have a minor preference for a more old school approach that I think works a bit better.

The trouble with a returned `tuple` going into a structured binding is that you have to get the structured binding names and their order right every time the lambda is invoked.  In this case that gives you five different opportunities to mess up.  To make things worse, this `tuple` contains two values of the same type.  So you could swap the names in the structured binding and the compiler will not offer you any kind of warning.

The way to avoid this is to replace the lambda \ `tuple` \ structured binding with a local `struct`.  The `struct` constructor can take the arguments you were passing to the lambda.  You must name the members of the `struct`, so there's no longer a concern about getting the names right in the structured binding.  The `struct` might look like this:
```
        // A struct that mints an NFT and creates a sell offer then returns
        // the NFT ID and the index of the offer.
        struct MintAndCreateSellOffer
        {
            uint256 nftID;
            uint256 sellOfferIndex;

            MintAndCreateSellOffer(
                test::jtx::Env& env,
                test::jtx::Account const& acct,
                STAmount const amt)
            {
                // acct mints a NFT
                nftID = token::getNextID(env, acct, 0u, tfTransferable);
                env(token::mint(acct, 0u), txflags(tfTransferable));
                env.close();

                // acct makes an sell offer
                sellOfferIndex = keylet::nftoffer(acct, env.seq(acct)).key;
                env(token::createOffer(
                    acct, nftID, amt), txflags(tfSellNFToken));
                env.close();
            }
        };
```
Using the `struct` would look like this:
```
            // alice mints an NFT and create a sell offer for 0 XRP
            MintAndCreateSellOffer const nft(env, alice, XRP(0));
            ...
                // Bob is able to accept the offer
                env(token::acceptSellOffer(bob, nft.sellOfferIndex));
```
Yet another alternative would be to keep the lambda, but ditch the `tuple` and the structured binding.  It turns out that even though the lambda returns the `nftID`, none of the callers care.  So you could just return the `sellOfferIndex` from the lambda and not have to worry about the order of the structure binding.

Any of these choices are viable.  But I did want to make you aware that you have some options that might be more maintainable.

On that note, I found it easier to understand a simpler test case:
```
// Bob has enough reserve for the Transaction Fees (10 drops) and the reserve for a new NFTokenPage
env(pay(env.master, bob, incReserve + drops(10)));
env.close();
env(token::acceptSellOffer(bob, sellOfferIndex));
env.close();

BEAST_EXPECT(ownerCount(env, bob) == 1);
```

If I have exactly 20 XRP, and I submit a transaction (eg. CreateOffer) that's going to take up a certain amount of reserve, it usually looks like:
```
        XRPAmount reserve =
            sb.fees().accountReserve(sleCreator->getFieldU32(sfOwnerCount) + 1);

        if (mPriorBalance < reserve){
           ...
        }

```
Here, I believe `mPriorBalance` will equal exactly to 20 XRP. So the comparison here is done before the transaction fee is deducted. This means that after this transaction is executed, the user will actually have 19.999990 XRP because 10 drops is deducted from the user's balance.

In this PR, we are comparing the balance that has already deducted tx fee. So in the example, buyerBalance would equal to 19.999990 XRP instead of 20XRP
